    "trajectory_id": "faraday_traj_005",
    "task": "Find information about the discovery of self-induction in Series IX, including Mr. Jenkin's observation, the copper wire used in §1064, and what physiological effects were produced.",
    "chunks_required": ["faraday_016"],
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "type": "plan",
        "thought": "I need chunk faraday_016 which covers Series IX §1048–1064, the self-induction discovery with Jenkin's observation and the 132-foot wire experiment."
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "type": "retrieve",
        "query": "Jenkin self-induction shock electro-magnet 132 feet copper wire convulse frog Series IX",
        "retrieved_chunk_ids": ["faraday_016"],
        "rationale": "faraday_016 covers Series IX §1048–1064, the self-induction discovery."
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "type": "extract",
        "chunk_id": "faraday_016",
        "extracted_facts": [
          "Jenkin's observation: short wire → no shock on breaking; electro-magnet wire → strong shock on breaking",
          "§1064: copper wire 0.18 inch diameter and 132 feet long, laid on laboratory floor",
          "On making: no sensible spark; on breaking: bright spark",
          "Physiological: 'powerful shocks upon the tongue and gums'; convulsed a flounder, an eel, or a frog"
        ]
      },
      {
        "step": 4,
        "type": "synthesise",
        "answer": "Self-induction was discovered by Faraday in Series IX through two complementary experiments. First, Mr. Jenkin communicated the observation that an ordinary short wire connecting a single pair of metals produces no shock when the circuit is broken, but the wire surrounding an electromagnet produces a strong shock each time contact is broken — the first evidence of back-EMF. Faraday then investigated self-induction in a plain wire: in §1064 he laid out 'a copper wire, 0.18 of an inch in diameter, and 132 feet in length' on the laboratory floor. On making contact no sensible spark appeared, but on breaking contact 'a bright one' appeared. Connecting silver plates to the wire extremities and placing the tongue between them produced 'powerful shocks upon the tongue and gums'; the wire was also powerful enough to convulse a flounder, an eel, or a frog."
      }
    ],
    "evaluation_rubric": {
