    "trajectory_id": "faraday_traj_007",
    "task": "Describe Faraday's discovery that ice is a non-conductor of electricity and the law he derived in §394–395. What other substance did he use to show the law was general?",
    "chunks_required": ["faraday_011"],
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "type": "plan",
        "thought": "I need chunk faraday_011 covering Series IV §380–395, the ice non-conductor discovery and the law of liquefaction."
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "type": "retrieve",
        "query": "ice non-conductor law liquefaction chloride of lead 150 plates Series IV",
        "retrieved_chunk_ids": ["faraday_011"],
        "rationale": "faraday_011 covers Series IV §380–395."
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "type": "extract",
        "chunk_id": "faraday_011",
        "extracted_facts": [
          "Ice discovered to be a non-conductor: thin film stops electricity even from 150 pairs of four-inch plates",
          "All chemical decomposition ceases when ice is interposed",
          "§394: law of liquefaction — conducting power acquired on melting, lost on freezing",
          "§395: verified for chloride of lead: fused it conducted; solid it did not"
        ]
      },
      {
        "step": 4,
        "type": "synthesise",
        "answer": "In Series IV Faraday was working with ice-based experiments when he found that even a thin film of ice interposed in the circuit of a very powerful voltaic battery stopped all current flow and chemical decomposition entirely. He tested with batteries of up to 150 pairs of four-inch plates — still no sensible electricity passed through the ice. In §394 he generalised this to a law: liquefaction confers conducting power, and congelation (freezing) removes it. He tested the law immediately on another substance: in §395 he fused chloride of lead and found it conducted electricity well; when solid it did not. This confirmed the law was not peculiar to water but was a general principle governing the conducting state of matter."
      }
    ],
